Real-World BIOS Attacks
1998 – Chernobyl (CIH) → Overwrote BIOS on systems with specific chipsets, making them unbootable.
2011 – Mebromi → First known BIOS-based rootkit targeting Award BIOS, loaded malicious code before OS.

Day-4: 
Virtualization

Virtual Machine (VM) → A computer simulated by software.
Virtual Memory → Part of your storage space used as if it were RAM.
Virtual Network → A network created in software that behaves like a physical network.
Virtual Reality → A computer-generated environment you can interact with.

Bare Metal (Traditional Infrastructure)
No virtualization. Applications run directly on hardware.

Pros: Maximum performance.
Cons: Poor scalability, inefficient resource utilization.

Virtual Machine:
Purpose: Run multiple operating systems on the same physical hardware.
Achieved via: Hypervisors.

Main Functions
Create and run virtual machines
Allocate hardware resources (CPU, RAM, storage, network) to each VM
Isolate VMs so one VM can’t directly affect another
Control and monitor VM performance

Virtualization vs Containerization
Virtualization → Provided by hypervisors → Creates VMs (each with a full guest OS).

Containerization → Provided by container engines (e.g., Docker, Podman, containerd) → Creates isolated app environments that share the host OS kernel.

Hypervisors virtualize hardware; container engines virtualize the application environment.
Hypervisors virtualize hardware, Docker virtualizes the software environment for apps

